# Multi-Creator configuration. Copy to .env.local for local work, or set these
# in Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables for a deployment.
#
# Nothing here is needed for the rest of the app: sign-in, the feed, chat,
# credits and single-player Create all talk to api.nanogram.app directly and
# need no configuration at all.

# --- Rooms: storage and realtime --------------------------------------------
# Supabase provides both. Create a project at supabase.com, then:
#
#   1. SQL Editor → paste supabase/schema.sql → Run. (Safe to re-run.)
#   2. Settings → API → copy the values below.
#
# SUPABASE_URL                 Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefgh.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY    Service role key. SERVER ONLY — it bypasses row
#                              level security, so it must never be sent to a
#                              browser or committed anywhere. Every read and
#                              write goes through our API, which holds this.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET          Settings → API → JWT Settings. Used to mint the
#                              short-lived tokens browsers use to subscribe to
#                              Realtime. Without it, storage still works and
#                              the client falls back to the SSE endpoint.
#
# Why Supabase rather than a key-value store: Realtime pushes. A polled backend
# charges the same for a busy room and a silent one, and rooms are mostly
# silent. Here an event insert is itself the broadcast, and presence (who is
# online) runs over the realtime channel without touching the database at all —
# so an idle room with people sitting in it costs nothing.
#
# Free tier at time of writing: 200 concurrent realtime connections and 2M
# realtime messages/month, 500MB database. Verify current limits before
# relying on them.
#
# Leave these unset locally and rooms fall back to an in-process Map plus the
# SSE endpoint, which is enough to click through the whole feature on one dev
# server. That fallback CANNOT work on Vercel: every invocation is isolated, so
# rooms would vanish between requests. The Rooms tab warns when it is live.
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# --- Offline building (optional) --------------------------------------------
# Encrypts a host's stored Nanogram refresh token (AES-256-GCM) so a room can
# keep building while the host is away. Any string of 32+ characters; it is
# hashed to a 256-bit key. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID()+crypto.randomUUID())"
#
# Without this, arming offline building is refused outright rather than
# silently storing credentials in the clear. Changing it later makes existing
# stored credentials permanently unreadable, which safely disarms every room.
#
# Understand the tradeoff before setting it: a stored refresh token is full
# access to that host's Nanogram account, so a leak of your database plus this
# key is a real account compromise. It is opt-in per room, expires on its own,
# and the host can delete it at any time.
ROOM_DELEGATION_KEY=

# --- Desktop builds only ----------------------------------------------------
# The Tauri app is not served by anything, so it has no /api of its own. Point
# it at a deployment to use Multi-Creator from the desktop app; leave it unset
# and the Rooms tab explains that the feature lives on the web.
# VITE_ROOMS_API=https://your-deployment.vercel.app/api
